H2: Known Faults - MINI Countryman
P: The faults below are split by generation: the first-generation car (2010-2017) and the second-generation car (2017-2024). We have no reliability source for the third-generation car (2024 on), so nothing here applies to it.
P: What Car? says the 2017-2024 Countryman finished with a solid overall score of 95.4%. MINI as a brand finished second among the manufacturers featured, with a score of 96.4%. For the first-generation car, Carbuyer says readers who have driven it are a fairly happy bunch, mainly rating it as reliable with only minor problems.
H3: Timing Chain
P: HIGH ú 2010-2017 petrol and diesel; 2017-2024 diesel ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says petrol Countrymen of 2010-2017 have been known to suffer stretching timing chains if they are run low on oil. Carbuyer says diesels made before March 2011 have been known to suffer broken timing chains, effectively ruining the engine. On the 2017-2024 car, What Car? says diesel owners should be wary of chain stretch. Autocar says the B47 diesel still suffers from timing chain issues, where the chain can stretch or snap, and that it is a big fix because the engine needs to come out to reach the chain. Ask for the service history and check the oil level yourself before you buy.
H3: Oil Pump Solenoid Valve Leaks
P: MONITOR ú 2010-2017 ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says Countrymen of this generation are known to suffer oil leaks, often from the oil pump solenoid valve. Look under the car and around the engine for fresh oil when you view one.
H3: Clutch
P: MONITOR ú 2010-2017 ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says that on an All4 model with a manual gearbox you should pay close attention to the clutch bite point. Carbuyer says pre-2012 cars can suffer premature clutch wear, so look out for any judder, slipping (where the engine seems to lose power) or reluctance to change gear. Test every gear on your drive.
H3: Engine Mounts and Crankshaft Sensors
P: MONITOR ú 2017-2024 ú Get a quote
P: What Car?'s reliability trends for the 2017-2024 car highlight occasional issues with engine mounts and crankshaft sensors, which can trigger limp mode. Autocar says the lower mount is typically the culprit and a new mount is the only cure.
H3: Turbo Wastegate Actuator
P: MONITOR ú 2017-2024 ú Get a quote
P: Autocar says turbo issues aren't unknown on the 2017-2024 car, and that the wastegate actuator is the main source of most of them. Have any fault codes read before you agree to a new turbo.
H3: Gear Selector After Liquid Spills
P: MINOR ú 2017-2024 ú œ1500 repair reported (What Car?)
P: What Car? says liquid spills near the central console can seep into the gear shifter circuitry, triggering an expensive œ1500 repair for a moisture detector fault. It also notes that some owners found parts, such as electric tailgate struts or interior modules, costly to fix once the warranty expires. Keep drinks away from the console.
H3: Electric Tailgate Struts
P: MINOR ú 2017-2024 ú Costly after warranty
P: Autocar says you should make sure the electric tailgate opens and closes properly, as the struts or actuator motor can fail, meaning it opens only halfway. What Car? says some owners found tailgate struts costly to fix once the warranty expired. Open and close the tailgate several times on a viewing.
H3: Early Car Niggles
P: MINOR ú Built before the 2014 facelift ú Get a quote
P: Carbuyer lists other problems reported before the 2014 facelift: some niggling rattles, an engine fan continuously running after the engine has been switched off and a failed temperature sensor.
H3: DVSA Safety Recalls
P: DVSA's recall data lists several Countryman recalls, including these.
LI: For certain vehicles BMW Group has developed a package of measures (hardware and software) for the emission control system of the MINI R60/R61
LI: On affected vehicles either one or both of the outer most rear seats may not have been manufactured correctly and the sensitivity sensor may be deformed
LI: On affected vehicles the front seat belts may have damage to the torsion bar caused during manufacture
LI: On affected vehicles the ground connection cable on the 48V power supply control unit may not have been torqued to the correct specification
LI: On affected vehicles the screws connecting the front end to the vehicles body may have been tightened to a torque level lower than required
P: To see whether a car has an open DVSA safety recall, use our recall check .
